            <ArticleTitle>Renal Cell Carcinoma in Renal Transplantation: The Case for Surveillance.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1779-1782</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0041-1345(17)30554-7</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.transproceed.2017.06.031</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Between January 2013 and September 2015, 135 consecutive renal transplant patients were screened prospectively with ultrasound for renal cell carcinoma (RCC).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Eighteen ultrasound abnormalities were identified with 4 solid lesions detected. Fifty-six other patients were screened retrospectively by referring nephrology groups, with 6 additional malignancies found.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">As a result of our data, we recommend and have instituted annual ultrasound screening of native kidneys in all renal transplant patients.</AbstractText>
